Why GPLv3
---------
Tether links org.whispersystems:signal-protocol-java and org.whispersystems:curve25519-java,
both of which are GPLv3. They are not distributed with this source tree, but they are
resolved at build time and linked into any application built against Tether, so the
distributed combination carries GPLv3 obligations. Licensing Tether itself under GPLv3
states that plainly instead of leaving a consumer to discover it from a transitive
dependency.

Upstream work
-------------
The protocol layers under tether-core (transport framing, the Noise handshake, the
binary-XML codec and its token dictionaries, ADV pairing, and the media key derivation)
are a Kotlin port of whatsmeow (https://github.com/tulir/whatsmeow), and
tether-core/src/main/proto/WAProto.proto is vendored from the same project. whatsmeow is
licensed under the Mozilla Public License 2.0.

MPL-2.0 permits distribution of a Larger Work under a Secondary License, and section 1.12
names the GNU GPL among those, so the ported code may be conveyed under GPLv3 provided the
MPL's own terms continue to be met for the covered files. This attribution is part of
meeting them.

Consuming Tether
----------------
GPLv3 is not LGPL: an application that links Tether must itself be distributable under
GPLv3. That constraint comes from the Signal libraries above, not from a choice to be
restrictive, and it would apply through them whatever licence this project chose.
